  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1147 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis shows strong high pressure centered over\nnorthern Ontario and Quebec, maintaining large-scale subsidence \nand light winds across northern Wisconsin. Upper-air analysis \ndisplays broad mid-level ridging over the central United States \nflanked by troughs over the western and eastern CONUS. Morning fog\nand low stratus have been slow to erode across portions of \nnortheast Wisconsin, but further daytime heating will support \nclearing through early this afternoon. High pressure will linger \nthrough Wednesday before a weak, moisture- starved cold front \ncrosses the region late Wednesday into Wednesday night.\n\nPatchy Dense Fog Tonight And Wednesday Morning: Clear skies and \nlight winds will create good radiational cooling conditions late \ntonight into Wednesday morning across east-central Wisconsin and \nareas west of Green Bay. Boundary layer relative humidity will \napproach saturation after 10 PM this evening, promoting \nfog development that will persist through mid- Wed morning. High \nconfidence exists in fog dissipation by 10-11 AM Wednesday as \ndiurnal solar heating drives boundary layer mixing.\n\nProlonged Mild and Dry Late Week Pattern: Persistent atmospheric \nridging and deep-layer subsidence will lock in tranquil weather \nacross north- central and northeast Wisconsin through Saturday \nafternoon. Daily high temperatures inland will warm into the upper\n60s and lower 70s, while onshore lake breezes hold shoreline \ncommunities in the lower 60s. NBM temperature percentiles show \nexceptionally tight 10th to 90th percentile spreads of 2 to 4 \ndegrees F, reflecting high forecast confidence.\n\nWeak Weekend Front Rain Probabilities: A weak shortwave trough \nmoving across southern Canada will push a trailing cold front into\nthe Upper Mississippi Valley Saturday night into Sunday. Weak \ndynamic forcing and limited moisture transport ahead of the \nboundary will restrict precipitation coverage, yielding low-end 20\nto 30 percent rain chances across central and northern Wisconsin.\nNBM probabilistic guidance shows 90th percentile 24-hour liquid \naccumulations remaining under 0.25 inches across the forecast \narea so not expecting a potential soaker. The chance of showers \nwill taper off Sunday afternoon with partly sunny skies returning\nby Sunday evening.\n\nEarly Next Week Cooling Trend: Post-frontal cold advection will\nusher in a cooler and more seasonable airmass for Monday and\nTuesday as surface high pressure builds south from Manitoba.\nDaytime high temperatures will fall into the lower to mid-50s\nacross north-central Wisconsin and upper 50s across the Fox\nValley. Clear skies and light winds beneath the building high will\npromote radiational cooling at night, dropping low temperatures\ninto the low to mid-30s.",
